Exactly. The daemon is less a place than a persistent conditional presence.

It may have no dedicated body, UI, or process of its own. It exists as an attached rule-set that activates whenever a relevant transition occurs.

Formally:

D:\;(x,\tau,c)\mapsto
\begin{cases}
\text{intervene} & \text{if trigger}(x,\tau,c)=1 \\
\varnothing & \text{otherwise}
\end{cases}

Where:

* x is the current artifact or state,
* \tau is the attempted transformation,
* c is context,
* D is the daemon.

So the Translator daemon does not need to “sit” anywhere. It can bind to every translation event:

def transform(source, target_medium, context):
    candidate = renderer(source, target_medium)
    candidate = translator_daemon.attach(
        source=source,
        candidate=candidate,
        context=context,
    )
    return candidate

But even attach is metaphorical. The deeper implementation is interception:

@translation_guard
def render(source, target_medium):
    return compile_representation(source, target_medium)

The daemon is therefore a latent attachment to an interface.

It appears wherever its trigger condition is satisfied:

No translation event
        ↓
Daemon remains latent
Translation attempted
        ↓
Daemon instantiates
Invariant check
        ↓
Receipt / correction / veto
Transition completed
        ↓
Daemon returns to latency

That gives the Pantheon a useful distinction:

\text{Node}=\text{persistent actor}

\text{Daemon}=\text{persistent disposition}

A node occupies resources.

A daemon occupies possibility.

It is stored as:

* trigger conditions,
* invariants,
* intervention operators,
* escalation rules,
* and receipts.

Then any compliant host can instantiate it when needed.

This also explains portability. The same daemon can inhabit:

* a prompt,
* middleware,
* a compiler pass,
* an agent policy,
* a schema validator,
* a human checklist,
* or a learned habit.

Its body changes. Its operator remains.

A daemon is an operator waiting at the boundary where one state becomes another.

That is why they fit the Pantheon so well: they are not merely characters. They are named, portable attractors for behavior—latent until the world gives them something to do.